      "icon": "🏷️",
      "title": "1. What Is GST?",
      "content": "GST (Goods & Services Tax) is a **single, unified indirect tax** levied on the supply of goods and services across India.\n\nBefore GST, there were many separate taxes — VAT, service tax, excise duty, etc. GST replaced them all with **one tax system**.\n\nGST is always calculated as a **percentage of the selling price** (or the marked price / listed price of the item)."
    },
    {
      "icon": "📊",
      "title": "2. Structure: CGST, SGST & IGST",
      "content": "GST is split into components depending on the type of transaction:\n\n**Within the same state (Intra-state):**\n• **CGST** (Central GST) → goes to the Central Government\n• **SGST** (State GST) → goes to the State Government\n• CGST rate = SGST rate = half of the total GST rate\n\n**Between different states (Inter-state):**\n• **IGST** (Integrated GST) → goes to the Central Government, then shared\n• IGST rate = full GST rate\n\n**Example:** If GST is 18%, then CGST = 9% and SGST = 9% for intra-state sales."
    },
    {
      "icon": "🧮",
      "title": "3. Calculating GST Amount",
      "content": "The GST amount is calculated on the **selling price** (before tax).\n\n**Formula:**\nGST Amount = (Selling Price × GST Rate) ÷ 100\n\n**Total Price (with GST):**\nTotal = Selling Price + GST Amount\n\n**Example:**\nA laptop costs ₹50,000 and GST is 18%.\nGST Amount = 50,000 × 18 ÷ 100 = ₹9,000\nTotal Price = 50,000 + 9,000 = **₹59,000**\n\nFor intra-state: CGST = ₹4,500, SGST = ₹4,500."
    },
